1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,560
Only 22% say that's unfair.

2
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:04,280
So this is one where you're really spelling it out

3
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:05,520
for people.

4
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:08,560
You're saying, here's the raise, here's the inflation.

5
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:11,520
And yet, people regard these as being very different.

6
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:13,600
Seems like there's something, for most people,

7
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:18,600
there's a moral barrier at the actual cut in your base pay.

8
00:00:19,840 --> 00:00:22,480
And again, so there is some wiggle room in a few ways,

9
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:26,040
like most people regard it as less unfair to slash a bonus.

10
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:29,060
But the thing is, the base hourly pay rate,

11
00:00:29,060 --> 00:00:31,600
that's the thing where people really freak out.

12
00:00:32,440 --> 00:00:35,520
So it's also considered not that unfair

13
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:37,440
to cut everybody's hours

14
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:39,600
while giving them the same wage per hour.

15
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:40,420
That's okay.

16
00:00:41,720 --> 00:00:44,280
But not okay to have them work the same number of hours

17
00:00:44,280 --> 00:00:48,280
for a smaller pay, which is satanic.

18
00:00:48,280 --> 00:00:49,360
It's just very evil.

19
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:53,560
Now, a very reasonable or honest reaction is to say,

20
00:00:53,560 --> 00:00:55,120
well, who cares about fairness?

21
00:00:55,120 --> 00:00:56,400
We're not in the fairness business,

22
00:00:56,400 --> 00:00:58,080
we're in the making money business.

23
00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:00,040
What difference does it make?

24
00:01:00,040 --> 00:01:00,960
But there, the answer is,

25
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:04,040
there's also evidence, surprisingly,

26
00:01:04,040 --> 00:01:07,800
that worker disgruntled hurts productivity.

27
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:11,440
When workers are unhappy, they are less productive.

28
00:01:12,520 --> 00:01:15,240
All right, so if you saw the seminar,

29
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:20,240
I think it was actually fall of 2016.

30
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:22,360
So this is Truman Buley, who has a book called

31
00:01:22,360 --> 00:01:24,740
Why Wages Don't Fall During Recession.

32
00:01:24,740 --> 00:01:28,320
So it was not the best seminar I've ever seen.

33
00:01:28,320 --> 00:01:30,520
He wouldn't even speak up to an audible volume.

34
00:01:30,520 --> 00:01:33,360
So if you were there and felt cheated,

35
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:34,680
we'll all say, look, the book's better.

36
00:01:34,680 --> 00:01:36,760
It's a lot, the book is really good.

37
00:01:36,760 --> 00:01:38,920
It's well-written, it's insightful.

38
00:01:38,920 --> 00:01:40,720
I was not happy with that seminar.

39
00:01:44,080 --> 00:01:46,000
I was in the front row, I couldn't hear.

40
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:47,840
And no matter how many times I asked him to speak louder,

41
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:49,000
he just wouldn't do it.

42
00:01:50,320 --> 00:01:51,160
Maybe you should have told me

43
00:01:51,160 --> 00:01:54,480
that you had a sub-audible volume.

44
00:01:54,480 --> 00:01:56,440
Or at least, if you told me that,

45
00:01:56,440 --> 00:01:57,680
I guess I could have gotten a microphone.

46
00:01:57,680 --> 00:01:59,440
Anyway, that was the,

47
00:01:59,440 --> 00:02:01,480
it was a long run, I really was embarrassed in the end.

48
00:02:01,480 --> 00:02:02,920
But the book, I stand by the book.

49
00:02:02,920 --> 00:02:04,340
The book's great.

50
00:02:04,340 --> 00:02:06,240
And he spent a lot of time talking to people

51
00:02:06,240 --> 00:02:08,980
actually who are involved in employment.

52
00:02:08,980 --> 00:02:11,280
And one of the most standard things they'll say

53
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:15,200
is if you cut workers' nominal base pay, it hurts morale.

54
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:18,280
All right, and again, well, why,

55
00:02:18,280 --> 00:02:21,240
well, if they cut their, if they don't work as hard,

56
00:02:21,240 --> 00:02:23,000
I'll just cut their pay again.

57
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,160
Yeah, but they'll cut their productivity again,

58
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:26,000
and there will be the spiral.

59
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:27,720
And here's the key thing.

60
00:02:27,720 --> 00:02:30,400
When you go and cut workers' pay,

61
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:33,000
what is obvious is that your workers,

62
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:35,280
on average, are less productive.

63
00:02:35,280 --> 00:02:36,880
What is generally not so obvious

64
00:02:36,880 --> 00:02:39,380
is which workers are slacking.

65
00:02:41,080 --> 00:02:43,960
So again, the story is not that the individual worker

66
00:02:43,960 --> 00:02:45,880
says, hey, you know how you cut my pay?

67
00:02:45,880 --> 00:02:47,800
Well, I'm gonna retaliate by not working hard

68
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:49,160
during the recession.

69
00:02:49,160 --> 00:02:51,940
That's a pretty good way to get fired right away.

70
00:02:51,940 --> 00:02:55,620
Instead, what workers usually do is silently,

71
00:02:55,620 --> 00:02:56,960
oh, come on, man, I just got fired.

72
00:02:56,960 --> 00:02:59,640
I'm gonna get my wages and go from there.

73
00:02:59,640 --> 00:03:01,440
And there's a lot of people like this,

74
00:03:01,440 --> 00:03:03,960
all of them going and trying to,

75
00:03:05,200 --> 00:03:07,400
at minimum, they just don't wanna give you

76
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:09,160
anything extra anymore.

77
00:03:09,160 --> 00:03:11,120
And a lot of them actually are looking for some way

78
00:03:11,120 --> 00:03:13,080
to get revenge, to get back at the man.

79
00:03:14,120 --> 00:03:16,400
All right, so the real problem then

80
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:18,160
is an employer goes and cuts pay by 20%,

81
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:19,840
and the productivity of the workforce

82
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:22,560
goes and falls by, say, 25%.

83
00:03:22,560 --> 00:03:24,320
And then if you say, well, just fire the workers

84
00:03:24,320 --> 00:03:27,200
who reduced the productivity,

85
00:03:27,200 --> 00:03:28,040
that's like, well, I don't know.

86
00:03:28,040 --> 00:03:30,640
It just seemed like the whole group turned against me.

87
00:03:31,820 --> 00:03:33,120
And if that is your situation,

88
00:03:33,120 --> 00:03:35,440
there's a reason to not cut the pay of the group.

89
00:03:36,880 --> 00:03:38,560
And again, what really upsets people, of course,

90
00:03:38,560 --> 00:03:40,320
is if you go and fire some people

91
00:03:40,320 --> 00:03:41,960
and then replace them with some other cheaper,

92
00:03:41,960 --> 00:03:43,720
where there's some cheaper replacements,

93
00:03:43,720 --> 00:03:44,800
because the people who are fired

94
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:47,960
are generally the friends of the other workers.

95
00:03:47,960 --> 00:03:50,160
Now, why not go and fire the entire workforce

96
00:03:50,160 --> 00:03:51,720
and replace it with new people?

97
00:03:52,680 --> 00:03:54,160
The main problem here seems to be

98
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:56,580
that the way people learn jobs is by practice,

99
00:03:56,580 --> 00:03:58,320
and the best kind of practice is practice

100
00:03:58,320 --> 00:04:01,440
under the tutelage of someone who already does that job.

101
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:03,840
So it's not safe to go and fire

102
00:04:03,840 --> 00:04:05,660
the entire workforce generally.

103
00:04:05,660 --> 00:04:08,920
Rupert Murdoch actually famously did this.

104
00:04:08,920 --> 00:04:12,280
Rupert Murdoch actually went and,

105
00:04:12,280 --> 00:04:14,720
he was sick of dealing with the printers' unions,

106
00:04:14,720 --> 00:04:19,520
so he surreptitiously went and created totally new factories

107
00:04:19,520 --> 00:04:22,460
like hundreds of miles from the existing ones

108
00:04:22,460 --> 00:04:24,760
and hired a totally new workforce,

109
00:04:24,760 --> 00:04:29,200
and then he provoked the existing ones into striking,

110
00:04:29,200 --> 00:04:30,440
and then he just shut the factories,

111
00:04:30,440 --> 00:04:31,780
and that was the end of it.

112
00:04:31,780 --> 00:04:33,760
And he said, yeah, and like, we'll strike.

113
00:04:33,760 --> 00:04:36,120
Well, you strike at the closed factories,

114
00:04:36,120 --> 00:04:37,200
and no one here anymore,

115
00:04:37,200 --> 00:04:38,480
nothing, I'm never gonna be open again,

116
00:04:38,480 --> 00:04:40,120
so strike there all you want,

117
00:04:40,120 --> 00:04:42,360
but fine, then we'll go strike at the new ones.

118
00:04:42,360 --> 00:04:45,240
If you wanna go and move 500 miles away from your home,

119
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:47,760
I deliberately found a place as far away in England

120
00:04:47,760 --> 00:04:51,040
as one can get from your current English location.

121
00:04:51,040 --> 00:04:53,240
All right, you know the size of England?

122
00:04:53,240 --> 00:04:55,360
That's the distance between where you are

123
00:04:55,360 --> 00:04:58,080
and where you'll have to go to strike, right?

124
00:04:58,080 --> 00:05:01,400
And that's where very few people were that committed

125
00:05:01,400 --> 00:05:04,500
to go and move 500 miles away to go and strike.

126
00:05:05,320 --> 00:05:06,160
Yes?

127
00:05:06,160 --> 00:05:06,980
Didn't Truman Buley find that,

128
00:05:06,980 --> 00:05:09,040
like, in a very small percentage of the cases,

129
00:05:09,040 --> 00:05:11,440
the employer, like, goes in front of the whole workforce

130
00:05:11,440 --> 00:05:13,120
and says, look, I have to cut nominal wages

131
00:05:13,120 --> 00:05:15,080
or else I'll have to fire some of you,

132
00:05:15,080 --> 00:05:17,480
and in that case, the productivity didn't shrink?

133
00:05:17,480 --> 00:05:18,540
Well, I think it was stronger.

134
00:05:18,540 --> 00:05:20,280
I think it was, I have to do it,

135
00:05:20,280 --> 00:05:22,920
or like, because we all have to say we're losing money.

136
00:05:22,920 --> 00:05:23,760
Oh, okay.

137
00:05:23,760 --> 00:05:24,800
So I think, so, you know, like,

138
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:26,440
we found is, yes, workers are more flexible

139
00:05:26,440 --> 00:05:28,840
when you actually say you are losing money.

140
00:05:28,840 --> 00:05:30,560
Is there a reason that hasn't caught on more

141
00:05:30,560 --> 00:05:31,400
during the session?

142
00:05:31,400 --> 00:05:32,760
Yes.

143
00:05:32,760 --> 00:05:33,600
Well, of course, a lot of firms,

144
00:05:33,600 --> 00:05:35,000
like, most firms are still making money.

145
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:36,200
They're just making less.

146
00:05:36,200 --> 00:05:37,040
You could lie to your employees.

147
00:05:37,040 --> 00:05:38,560
Yeah, you could lie.

148
00:05:38,560 --> 00:05:39,480
I guess I could find out.

149
00:05:39,480 --> 00:05:41,480
Yeah, so, I mean, that's sort of like

150
00:05:41,480 --> 00:05:42,320
part of the bigger question.

151
00:05:42,320 --> 00:05:43,760
Why isn't there just more lying in the world?

152
00:05:43,760 --> 00:05:45,440
Seems like people get away with it most of the time

153
00:05:45,440 --> 00:05:46,880
and that they're not punished as harshly

154
00:05:46,880 --> 00:05:50,440
as you would need to really make it not worthwhile.

155
00:05:51,480 --> 00:05:52,320
Yes.

156
00:05:52,320 --> 00:05:54,800
Yeah, a lot of that information's public domain, though,

157
00:05:54,800 --> 00:05:55,920
right, like, it's fake, public,

158
00:05:55,920 --> 00:05:57,480
they can't really lie about it.

159
00:05:57,480 --> 00:05:58,320
Maybe.

160
00:05:58,320 --> 00:05:59,680
Well, so, like, public, it's good.

161
00:05:59,680 --> 00:06:01,440
If you're not, as long as you're not a government company,

162
00:06:01,440 --> 00:06:04,000
you don't have to publicize salaries, do you?

163
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:05,680
George Mason has to publicize salaries,

164
00:06:05,680 --> 00:06:07,600
Freedom of Information Act.

165
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:08,440
But, yeah.

166
00:06:08,440 --> 00:06:09,600
In what country you are?

167
00:06:09,600 --> 00:06:10,440
Ah, so.

168
00:06:10,440 --> 00:06:11,880
In Scandinavia, probably.

169
00:06:11,880 --> 00:06:14,280
Ah, okay, so there, everybody, yes and no.

170
00:06:14,280 --> 00:06:15,120
Yeah, but, you know, the other thing is,

171
00:06:15,120 --> 00:06:18,320
it just may be that people are not that good at lying

172
00:06:18,320 --> 00:06:20,080
and not that good at keeping a secret.

173
00:06:20,080 --> 00:06:20,920
Yes.

174
00:06:20,920 --> 00:06:22,080
Are you aware of any data that shows

175
00:06:22,080 --> 00:06:24,600
that productivity goes down if it's a non-profit?

176
00:06:25,960 --> 00:06:28,920
I'm pretty sure that Bule did interview some non-profits.

177
00:06:28,920 --> 00:06:31,800
So, I mean, the non-profits are less likely

178
00:06:31,800 --> 00:06:34,480
to have any independent measure of productivity,

179
00:06:34,480 --> 00:06:35,960
but I just don't remember, so.

180
00:06:35,960 --> 00:06:38,520
It probably hasn't been studied.

181
00:06:38,520 --> 00:06:40,200
By anybody, you have to look at ones

182
00:06:40,200 --> 00:06:41,560
where there's some measure of productivity.

183
00:06:41,560 --> 00:06:44,040
Well, you know, like, a lot of non-profits

184
00:06:44,040 --> 00:06:45,680
just wouldn't have a good measure.

185
00:06:46,720 --> 00:06:47,560
Okay.

186
00:06:48,480 --> 00:06:49,520
All right, so anyway, it looks like efforts,

187
00:06:49,520 --> 00:06:51,360
partly about incentives, partly it's just about trust,

188
00:06:51,360 --> 00:06:55,000
and when you cut pay, then workers just get angry,

189
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:56,280
and then it's hard, so.

190
00:06:56,280 --> 00:06:58,120
I've had this argument with Tyler Cowen so many times

191
00:06:58,120 --> 00:07:00,280
where he keeps, at least in my mind,

192
00:07:00,280 --> 00:07:01,800
keeps just saying, look, that doesn't make any sense

193
00:07:01,800 --> 00:07:02,760
because he would just fire the people

194
00:07:02,760 --> 00:07:04,680
that cut the productivity, and I keep saying,

195
00:07:04,680 --> 00:07:06,240
the whole point is there's asymmetric regression

196
00:07:06,240 --> 00:07:08,360
on who exactly is doing it.

197
00:07:08,360 --> 00:07:10,440
It's hard to figure out who is trying,

198
00:07:10,440 --> 00:07:12,680
who is sticking it to the man and who isn't,

199
00:07:12,680 --> 00:07:14,240
because people sticking to the man don't usually say,

200
00:07:14,240 --> 00:07:15,880
hey, man, come over here.

201
00:07:15,880 --> 00:07:17,200
All right, all right, all right,

202
00:07:17,200 --> 00:07:18,680
you want to know about who's sticking it to you?

203
00:07:18,680 --> 00:07:20,200
Me, here's the stick, ha!

204
00:07:20,200 --> 00:07:21,640
All right, so usually don't do that.

205
00:07:21,640 --> 00:07:24,000
That is a good way to get fired during a session.

206
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:25,840
Instead, you stick it to the man

207
00:07:25,840 --> 00:07:27,200
when the man's not looking.

208
00:07:28,360 --> 00:07:30,680
And in that case, it can be, like,

209
00:07:30,680 --> 00:07:33,760
it seems like the story's pretty irretite.

210
00:07:33,760 --> 00:07:36,040
So, anyway, so Tyler does correctly say,

211
00:07:36,040 --> 00:07:37,560
well, there's, like, bonuses and other things,

212
00:07:37,560 --> 00:07:38,440
a bit more flexibility,

213
00:07:38,440 --> 00:07:40,480
but a lot of jobs don't have that style,

214
00:07:40,480 --> 00:07:42,400
so how does all that figure in?

215
00:07:43,800 --> 00:07:46,840
All right, okay, and let's see here.

216
00:07:46,840 --> 00:07:50,040
All right, now, so some people have actually asked employers,

217
00:07:50,040 --> 00:07:52,680
so how do workers respond to wage cuts versus layoffs?

218
00:07:52,680 --> 00:07:55,840
So not only do workers get really upset about wage cuts,

219
00:07:55,840 --> 00:07:59,160
layoffs, according to a lot of people, increase productivity.

220
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:03,360
When someone gets laid off, many people are scared,

221
00:08:03,360 --> 00:08:04,460
but they're not angry.

222
00:08:05,440 --> 00:08:07,920
They have a different negative emotion.

223
00:08:07,920 --> 00:08:09,600
So there's negative emotions all around,

224
00:08:09,600 --> 00:08:12,520
whereas anger causes you to try to hurt your boss,

225
00:08:12,520 --> 00:08:14,840
fear causes you to try to please your boss.

226
00:08:16,240 --> 00:08:19,200
As to why these are the emotions people feel,

227
00:08:19,200 --> 00:08:20,760
your guess is better than mine.

228
00:08:22,240 --> 00:08:24,320
If I were, you know, I honestly think I am at the point

229
00:08:24,320 --> 00:08:26,520
where if I, like, during a recession,

230
00:08:26,520 --> 00:08:29,920
like, I would be very grateful for whatever was going on.

231
00:08:29,920 --> 00:08:33,040
Like, if, like, I keep my job, I'm really happy here,

232
00:08:33,040 --> 00:08:38,040
so, but a lot of people have a lot more anger than I do.

233
00:08:42,200 --> 00:08:45,360
And then a funny anecdotal piece of evidence.

234
00:08:45,360 --> 00:08:48,480
So at UC Berkeley Economics Departments,

235
00:08:48,480 --> 00:08:52,200
I have it from insiders that there was a brief period

236
00:08:52,200 --> 00:08:55,880
in the early 90s when UC Berkeley did impose,

237
00:08:55,880 --> 00:08:57,880
like, a one or 2% pay cut on faculty,

238
00:08:58,840 --> 00:09:02,800
and overheard in the Econ Faculty Lounge

239
00:09:02,800 --> 00:09:04,480
were some very high-level theorists

240
00:09:04,480 --> 00:09:06,640
with nothing but contempt for psychology

241
00:09:06,640 --> 00:09:11,600
who were livid, fuming, slobbering

242
00:09:11,600 --> 00:09:13,880
over this one or 2% pay cut.

243
00:09:13,880 --> 00:09:16,680
These same guys, 10 years earlier,

244
00:09:16,680 --> 00:09:18,600
were getting much bigger real pay cuts

245
00:09:18,600 --> 00:09:22,040
during the high inflation, but were not upset.

246
00:09:23,960 --> 00:09:26,520
So even theorists ideologically committed

247
00:09:26,520 --> 00:09:28,520
to the idea that they are the same,

248
00:09:28,520 --> 00:09:31,360
some of the smartest economists in the country,

249
00:09:31,360 --> 00:09:35,200
were acting like normal human beings, for shame.

250
00:09:35,200 --> 00:09:36,040
All right?

251
00:09:36,040 --> 00:09:36,880
All right?

252
00:09:36,880 --> 00:09:39,400
And they did the fairness study.

253
00:09:39,400 --> 00:09:42,480
Did they also look at zero adjustment,

254
00:09:42,480 --> 00:09:45,160
but then some measure of positive inflation?

255
00:09:45,160 --> 00:09:46,000
I don't remember.

256
00:09:47,880 --> 00:09:48,800
Yeah, so we can evaluate that.

257
00:09:48,800 --> 00:09:51,080
So, I mean, I think actually you could just Google

258
00:09:51,080 --> 00:09:53,360
those exact numbers, like Google Scholar,

259
00:09:53,360 --> 00:09:56,000
and the original study should pop up.

260
00:09:56,000 --> 00:10:01,000
All right, and we will stop here, continue the next class.


